## arena.src -- Lume standard library: heap observability and arena
## (region) allocation over the native runtime (refs #404).
## Import with `use arena` (spec-level name `std.arena`; the bundler strips the
## `std.` prefix, lmod.src `modname_clean`).
##
## This module is a thin, honest wrapper over the runtime primitives that
## `compiler/stage1/lume/05_codegen.src` (and the ML-core
## `compiler/stage1/data_asmgen.lume`) emit when a program calls the
## `__lume_*` names below. The runtime owns the real memory; this module only
## gives it a documented Lume surface. Composition would be impossible here:
## a region cannot be built from str/int builtins, so these ARE new
## runtime builtins, wired exactly like the `__lume_rc_*`/`__lume_promise_*`
## memory-tier builtins (Track O).
##
## Real level, stated not hidden:
## - `heap_stats() -> {used, cap, allocs}` and `heap_peak_used() -> int` are
##   SHIPPED. `used` = live bytes on the runtime heap: the normal bump heap
##   PLUS every live arena's used bytes; `cap` = the configured LUME_HEAP_CAP
##   in bytes, or the total mapped heap (normal + arena regions) when no cap
##   is configured; `allocs` = allocation calls (`alloc` + `arena_alloc`).
##   `heap_peak_used()` is the high-water mark of `used` over the process
##   lifetime. After `arena_reset`, `used` drops immediately (the region is
##   reusable); `heap_peak_used()` keeps the high-water mark.
## - Arenas are real second heap regions: `arena_new(cap_hint)` carves a
##   dedicated `brk` region ABOVE the normal heap, so arena allocation
##   INTERLEAVES freely with ordinary boxed allocation (no LIFO discipline,
##   no scope restrictions). Up to 4 arenas are live at once; `arena_new`
##   fails closed beyond that. `cap_hint` (<= 0 means default 1 MiB) is a HARD
##   per-arena budget enforced at `arena_alloc`.
## - Stated limitation (real, fail-closed): while any arena is live, the
##   normal heap cannot grow past the lowest arena base -- the arena "pins
##   the heap ceiling". A normal allocation that would need to grow beyond
##   the pin fails closed with a clear diagnostic (exit 12); reset/destroy
##   the arenas to unpin. Arena regions are carved on demand and bounded by
##   LUME_HEAP_CAP like every other mapping.
## - `arena_alloc(arena, n)` is an O(1) bump: it returns the address of `n`
##   raw bytes as a boxed int. The address DANGLES after `arena_reset`/
##   `arena_destroy` (the next alloc reuses it); only the handle is stable
##   across a reset. Writing through the address needs the raw-memory
##   builtins (`mmio_volatile_write64`); the memory-peak tests touch cells
##   with it so the kernel really supplies the pages.
## - Fail-closed diagnostics go to stderr and exit with status 12 (the memory
##   error family, same as out-of-memory): heap cap exceeded, invalid
##   LUME_HEAP_CAP at startup, arena too-many/dead-handle/budget/size, heap
##   ceiling pinned by an arena. Observability and arenas are HOSTED-runtime
##   features; `@freestanding` kernel images keep their fixed heap and do not
##   get these builtins.
##
## Relationship to ownership.src: the O-track `arena_new`/`arena_alloc` there
## are a pure ACCOUNTING model of a region (records carrying {cap, used}),
## used to check `with alloc = arena` at compile time. This module is the
## real runtime arena. The names would collide in one bundle, so a program
## uses one or the other; the accounting model is for the checker, this one
## is for actual memory.
##
fn arena_new(cap_hint) = __lume_arena_new(cap_hint)
fn arena_alloc(arena, n) = __lume_arena_alloc(arena, n)
fn arena_reset(arena) = __lume_arena_reset(arena)
fn arena_destroy(arena) = __lume_arena_destroy(arena)
fn heap_stats() = __lume_heap_stats()
fn heap_peak_used() = __lume_heap_peak_used()
fn peak_rss_kb() = os_self_maxrss_kb()
fn child_peak_rss_kb() = os_last_child_maxrss_kb()
